In article <1102666605.639994.289310@z14g2000cwz.googlegroups.com>,
Michael Lehotay <mlehotay@gmail.com> wrote:
> Giving points for progress in the game is a very good idea. But how do
> you measure progress? By milestones reached (entering the Quest,
> getting the candelabrum, etc)? There would have to be more to it than
> that, otherwise the scoreboard would be filled with many entries having
> exactly the same number of points.

Hmm.

A good scoring system should:
- Reward progress towards the goal of ascending.
- Not allow arbitrarily high scores through repetition of the same
actions.
- Have a theoretical maximum score that is impossible to reach in
practice.
- Rarely result in ties.
- Encourage interesting behavior.

Perhaps structure the score as a combination of a number of different
elements:

- A fixed point award for each new level reached, with bonuses for
certain milestones.  Since the dungeon size is variable, adjust the
bonuses for the ends of branches so completing a branch always gives
the same score.  (40,000 points for the Dungeons of Doom, for example,
payable in installments of 100 points for each level with the remainder
awarded on reaching the Castle.)

- Bonuses for certain accomplishments on the way to ascention. 
(Completing the quest, defeating Vlad, ascending, etc.)  These might
offer a speed bonus; the faster you accomplish each step, the more
points you get.  This offers a certain tension between trying to
optimize for speed or non-ascention-related points.

- Experience score--a function of your total experience points,
exponentially declining past a certain point.  Going from level 1 to
level 20 will get you a nice bonus to your score.  Going from 30 to 50
probably won't be worth the bother.

- Carnage score--points awarded for each monster killed.  Killing many
types of monster gets you many points.  Killing many monsters of the
same type gets you exponentially fewer points with each one killed.

The points awarded for each type of monster exponentially decrease with
each one killed.  Your first jackal and your first Uruk-hai will both
give the same number of points; your 200th Uruk-hai will give almost
nothing.

- Wealth--points awarded for zorkmids of wealth owned at the end of the
game, decreasing exponentially with higher numbers of zorkmids.

- Exploration score--a pool of bonus points, awarded based on the
percentage of the dungeon which has been mapped, number of items
identified, etc.

- Exit multiplier--your final score is multiplied by a constant, based
on how you ended the game.  Ascention gives the best multiplier, while
quitting gives the worst.

- Additional small bonuses for interesting actions: First time you give
a gem to an aligned unicorn, #chatting with Izchak, being crowned,
taming creatures, and so forth.

